# Measure which layer misplaces the bottom-row target effect.
#
# Build with debug info first, or none of this resolves:
#
#   make -C theos RBPDBG=1 RBPDBGINFO=1
#
# Then, with the play screen up:
#
#   (lldb) command source /var/jb/var/mobile/rb458-targets.lldb
#   (lldb) continue
#
# The top row of targets aligns and the bottom row does not, so two sprites that should coincide
# have different y. Every sprite is placed through one of the two setters below, so breaking there
# and printing the owning batch with the position names the responsible layer directly: `bt 3`
# gives the caller and `this` distinguishes the batches.
#
# The commands are attached with -o rather than the interactive form. `breakpoint command add` with
# no -o opens an editor that waits for a line reading DONE, which never arrives from a sourced file
# and leaves the session hanging. --auto-continue lets the game keep running rather than stopping on
# every sprite.
#
# Expect a lot of output. Let it run a second or two, interrupt, and read back.

breakpoint set --name "ne::C_SPRITE_INSTANCING_2D::SetSpritePositionXY"
breakpoint command add -o "frame variable this nIndex x y" -o "bt 3" 1
breakpoint modify --auto-continue true 1

breakpoint set --name "ne::C_SPRITE_INSTANCING_2D::SetSpritePosition"
breakpoint command add -o "frame variable this nIndex position" -o "bt 3" 2
breakpoint modify --auto-continue true 2

# If the volume is unmanageable, disable one: `breakpoint disable 2`. If neither resolves, check
# `image lookup -t neGLESRenderer` first -- no output there means the build has symbols but no
# DWARF, and RBPDBGINFO was not set.
